### What is a linked list?

A linked list is a linear data structure made of nodes. Each node is a small object with two parts: the data it carries and a `next` reference to the following node. The list itself usually holds only a `head` reference that points to the first node. To reach any node you start at the head and follow `next` references one by one. The final node's `next` is `null`, which signals the end of the chain.

In a banking system, you can think of each node as a pending loan application. Rajesh submits five loan applications, and each application points to the next one in the queue.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LoanApplicationNode {
    String applicationId;
    BigDecimal loanAmount;
    LoanApplicationNode next;

    public LoanApplicationNode(String applicationId, BigDecimal loanAmount) {
        this.applicationId = applicationId;
        this.loanAmount = loanAmount;
        this.next = null;
    }
}
```

### How is a linked list different from an array?

The defining contrast is memory layout. An array stores elements in one continuous block, so element `i` is found instantly by computing an address offset, giving O(1) random access. A linked list stores nodes anywhere on the heap, so to reach the fifth node you must walk through the first four, giving O(n) access.

The trade-off is that inserting or deleting at the front of a linked list is O(1) because you just re-point a reference, whereas inserting at the front of an array is O(n) because every element must shift right.

| Feature | Array | Linked List |
|---|---|---|
| Memory layout | Contiguous block | Scattered on heap |
| Access by index | O(1) | O(n) |
| Insert at head | O(n) shift | O(1) re-point |
| Insert at tail | O(1) amortized | O(n) walk or O(1) with tail ref |
| Cache locality | Excellent | Poor |
| Memory overhead | None per element | One pointer per node |

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import com.bank.model.LoanApplicationNode;

public class LinkedListVsArray {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Array: O(1) access by index
        BigDecimal[] amounts = {new BigDecimal("50000"), new BigDecimal("100000")};
        System.out.println("Array index 1: " + amounts[1]);

        // Linked list: must walk from head
        LoanApplicationNode head = new LoanApplicationNode("LOAN-001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        head.next = new LoanApplicationNode("LOAN-002", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        LoanApplicationNode current = head;
        current = current.next; // walk one step to reach second node
        System.out.println("Linked list node 2: " + current.loanAmount);
    }
}
```

```text
Array index 1: 100000
Linked list node 2: 100000
```

### How do you build a simple singly linked list?

You create a node class and a list class that holds the `head` reference. The list provides methods to add nodes at the head, at the tail, and to print the chain.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class LoanApplicationList {
    private LoanApplicationNode head;
    private int size;

    public void addFirst(String id, BigDecimal amount) {
        LoanApplicationNode node = new LoanApplicationNode(id, amount);
        node.next = head;
        head = node;
        size++;
    }

    public void addLast(String id, BigDecimal amount) {
        LoanApplicationNode node = new LoanApplicationNode(id, amount);
        if (head == null) {
            head = node;
        } else {
            LoanApplicationNode current = head;
            while (current.next != null) {
                current = current.next;
            }
            current.next = node;
        }
        size++;
    }

    public void printAll() {
        LoanApplicationNode current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.println(current.applicationId + " : " + current.loanAmount);
            current = current.next;
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LoanApplicationList list = new LoanApplicationList();
        list.addLast("LOAN-001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        list.addLast("LOAN-002", new BigDecimal("200000"));
        list.addFirst("LOAN-003", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        list.printAll();
    }
}
```

```text
LOAN-003 : 75000
LOAN-001 : 50000
LOAN-002 : 200000
```

### What is a doubly linked list?

A doubly linked list adds a `prev` pointer to each node so you can traverse in both directions. Java's `java.util.LinkedList` is actually a doubly linked list internally. The trade-off is that each node uses more memory for the extra pointer, but you gain the ability to remove a node in O(1) when you already have a reference to it, because you can reach both neighbors.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class DoublyLinkedLoanNode {
    String applicationId;
    BigDecimal loanAmount;
    DoublyLinkedLoanNode prev;
    DoublyLinkedLoanNode next;

    public DoublyLinkedLoanNode(String applicationId, BigDecimal loanAmount) {
        this.applicationId = applicationId;
        this.loanAmount = loanAmount;
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.core;

import com.bank.model.DoublyLinkedLoanNode;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class DoublyLinkedDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DoublyLinkedLoanNode a = new DoublyLinkedLoanNode("LOAN-001", new BigDecimal("50000"));
        DoublyLinkedLoanNode b = new DoublyLinkedLoanNode("LOAN-002", new BigDecimal("100000"));
        DoublyLinkedLoanNode c = new DoublyLinkedLoanNode("LOAN-003", new BigDecimal("200000"));

        a.next = b; b.prev = a;
        b.next = c; c.prev = b;

        // Traverse forward
        System.out.println("Forward:");
        DoublyLinkedLoanNode current = a;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.println("  " + current.applicationId);
            current = current.next;
        }

        // Traverse backward from last node
        System.out.println("Backward:");
        current = c;
        while (current != null) {
            System.out.println("  " + current.applicationId);
            current = current.prev;
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Forward:
  LOAN-001
  LOAN-002
  LOAN-003
Backward:
  LOAN-003
  LOAN-002
  LOAN-001
```

### What about cache locality and performance?

Linked lists have poor cache locality because nodes are scattered across the heap. Modern CPUs load memory in cache lines, so accessing contiguous array elements is fast because neighboring elements are already in the cache. With a linked list, each `next` dereference can jump to a completely different memory region, causing a cache miss.

For pure iteration, an `ArrayList` is usually faster than a `LinkedList` in practice despite the same O(n) big-O, because of this cache behavior. Use a linked list when you need frequent O(1) insertions and deletions at known positions, not when you need fast sequential reads.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.List;

public class CacheLocalityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 1_000_000;

        List<Integer> arrayList = new ArrayList<>();
        List<Integer> linkedList = new LinkedList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            arrayList.add(i);
            linkedList.add(i);
        }

        long start = System.nanoTime();
        long sum1 = 0;
        for (int val : arrayList) sum1 += val;
        long arrayTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        start = System.nanoTime();
        long sum2 = 0;
        for (int val : linkedList) sum2 += val;
        long linkedTime = System.nanoTime() - start;

        System.out.println("ArrayList iteration:  " + arrayTime / 1_000_000 + " ms");
        System.out.println("LinkedList iteration: " + linkedTime / 1_000_000 + " ms");
    }
}
```

```text
ArrayList iteration:  5 ms
LinkedList iteration: 12 ms
```

### What are common mistakes with linked lists?

The most common mistake is losing the head reference. If you reassign `head` during a traversal instead of using a separate `current` pointer, you lose access to the beginning of the list permanently.

```java
// WRONG: moves head itself, losing the start of the list
while (head != null) {
    System.out.println(head.applicationId);
    head = head.next; // head is now lost!
}
// After this loop, head is null and the list is unreachable
```

```java
// CORRECT: uses a separate pointer, head stays intact
LoanApplicationNode current = head;
while (current != null) {
    System.out.println(current.applicationId);
    current = current.next;
}
// head still points to the first node
```

Another common mistake is forgetting to handle the empty list case. When `head` is `null`, calling `head.next` throws a `NullPointerException`.

```java
// WRONG: crashes on empty list
public BigDecimal getFirstAmount() {
    return head.loanAmount; // NullPointerException if head is null
}

// CORRECT: guard against null head
public BigDecimal getFirstAmount() {
    if (head == null) {
        throw new IllegalStateException("No pending loan applications");
    }
    return head.loanAmount;
}
```

### When should you use a linked list?

Use a linked list when you need frequent insertions and deletions at the front or at known positions in constant time. Queues and deques are natural fits. Do not use a linked list when you need fast random access by index or when you iterate sequentially over large datasets, because an array or `ArrayList` will outperform it due to cache locality.

In banking, a linked list works well for a pending-transaction queue where new transactions arrive at the front, processed transactions are removed from the front, and you rarely need to jump to the middle.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import com.bank.model.LoanApplicationNode;

public class PendingLoanQueue {
    private LoanApplicationNode head;

    public void enqueue(String id, BigDecimal amount) {
        LoanApplicationNode node = new LoanApplicationNode(id, amount);
        node.next = head;
        head = node; // O(1) insert at front
    }

    public LoanApplicationNode dequeue() {
        if (head == null) return null;
        LoanApplicationNode removed = head;
        head = head.next; // O(1) remove from front
        removed.next = null;
        return removed;
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should understand that `java.util.LinkedList` implements both `List` and `Deque` interfaces, making it usable as a list, a queue, or a double-ended queue. However, in almost all practical situations, `ArrayList` is preferred for `List` operations because of cache locality.

The real power of linked list knowledge is in coding interviews and in understanding the internals of data structures like `LinkedHashMap`, `ConcurrentLinkedQueue`, and skip lists. Knowing how to reverse a list, detect a cycle, merge sorted lists, and find the middle node with the fast-slow pointer technique are the core interview skills.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.LinkedList;
import java.util.Deque;

public class JavaLinkedListDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // java.util.LinkedList as a Deque
        Deque<String> approvalQueue = new LinkedList<>();
        approvalQueue.offerLast("LOAN-001");
        approvalQueue.offerLast("LOAN-002");
        approvalQueue.offerFirst("LOAN-PRIORITY"); // priority insert at front

        System.out.println("Process: " + approvalQueue.pollFirst()); // LOAN-PRIORITY
        System.out.println("Process: " + approvalQueue.pollFirst()); // LOAN-001
        System.out.println("Process: " + approvalQueue.pollFirst()); // LOAN-002
    }
}
```

```text
Process: LOAN-PRIORITY
Process: LOAN-001
Process: LOAN-002
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often follow up "What is a linked list?" with these questions:

- How do you reverse a singly linked list in place?
- How do you detect a cycle using Floyd's slow-fast pointer?
- How do you find the middle node in one pass?
- How do you merge two sorted linked lists?
- What is the difference between `ArrayList` and `LinkedList` in Java?
- When would you choose a linked list over an array in production?
